    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite passes (BUILD SUCCESS, 1/1 tests green).\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded duplicate-email rejection to owner creation with the smallest possible structural footprint: a **single new clause** in the existing CONFLICT condition of `addOwner` in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n```java\n|| (owner.getEmail() != null && owners.stream().anyMatch(existing -> owner.getEmail().equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getEmail())))\n```\n\n### Why this keeps structural impact low\n- **One file touched**, so the file multiplier stays at 1.\n- **One function changed** (`addOwner`), and only **one line added**. Since the cost model penalizes `changed_lines \u00d7 CC \u00d7 WMC_other`, and this class's `WMC_other` is large, minimizing the line count and avoiding a second changed function (or a new method \u2014 which would itself inherit the whole class as its `WMC_other`) was the cheapest option.\n- **Reused existing machinery**: the check piggybacks on the already-present `owners`/`anyMatch` pattern and the existing 409 return path, so no new control-flow structure or helper was needed.\n\n### Correctness notes\n- `Owner.setEmail` already lower-cases the stored value and the mapper auto-maps `email` from the DTO, so comparison is against lower-cased emails as the spec requires; `equalsIgnoreCase` makes it robust to any legacy mixed-case data.\n- The `owner.getEmail() != null` guard prevents a NPE and ensures owners with no email never collide with each other.",
